// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// agent_proposal.slint — the companion proposes, the person decides
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// One card, one grammar, everywhere the companion wants to change something.
//
// # Why this is a component and not a panel in each app
//
// The Rust side has had the approval primitive from the start: `Action::defers()` marks an
// action as one that cannot answer immediately, and `yos act` grades every action safe /
// standard / sensitive / dangerous. None of that reached the screen. Searching every .slint
// file in the OS for the word "approve" returned nothing.
//
// What Notes had instead was a bespoke panel: a text blob with Apply and Dismiss under it. The
// instinct was right -- Apply appends the text and deliberately leaves the note UNSAVED, so
// generated words have to be looked at before they are kept -- but it was one app's private
// answer to a question every app has. Sixteen private answers is how an OS ends up with
// sixteen different ideas of what "the computer wants to do something" looks like, which is
// the opposite of trustworthy.
//
// # What a proposal must say
//
// Three things, and it will not draw without them:
//
//   what it is        — the title, and the text itself
//   where it came from — provenance. "from this note", "from memory", "from your calendar".
//                        An answer with no stated source is a claim, and this OS does not
//                        make claims it cannot attribute.
//   what it will do   — the impact line. Empty means nothing changes and this is just an
//                        answer to read. Non-empty means something is about to move, and it
//                        says exactly what before the button is pressed.
//
// # Why there is no "Approve" button
//
// Because the verb should be the thing that happens. "Apply", "Replace", "Move 14 files",
// "Send". A button labelled Approve makes the person agree to something abstract; a button
// labelled with the change makes them agree to the change.

import { Theme } from "theme.slint";
import { Icon, Icons } from "icon.slint";
import { YButton } from "y_button.slint";
import { AppIdentity } from "app_color.slint";

export struct AgentProposal {
    // What this is, in three or four words: "Summary ready", "Drafted reply".
    title: string,
    // The proposal itself.
    body: string,
    // Where it came from. Required in spirit: a card with no source reads as a machine
    // asserting something, which is what this OS is trying not to be.
    source: string,
    // What changes if this is applied. Empty = nothing changes, this is only an answer.
    impact: string,
    // Applying cannot be undone by pressing the other button.
    destructive: bool,
    // The verb on the button. Empty falls back to "Apply".
    verb: string,
}

export component AgentProposalCard inherits Rectangle {
    in property <AgentProposal> data;
    in property <bool> working: false;

    callback apply();
    callback dismiss();

    height: body-col.preferred-height;
    border-radius: Theme.r-lg;
    background: Theme.glass-card;
    border-width: 1px;
    // A proposal that changes something is outlined in the app's colour so it reads as the
    // companion speaking rather than as another panel of the app's own chrome.
    border-color: root.data.impact != ""
        ? AppIdentity.accent.transparentize(60%)
        : Theme.glass-border;

    body-col := VerticalLayout {
        padding: Theme.sp-4;
        spacing: Theme.sp-3;
        alignment: start;

        // ── Who is talking, and about what ──
        HorizontalLayout {
            spacing: Theme.sp-2;
            alignment: start;

            Rectangle {
                width: 22px;
                height: 22px;
                border-radius: Theme.r-sm;
                background: AppIdentity.tint;
                Icon {
                    commands: Icons.spark;
                    size: 14px;
                    tint: AppIdentity.accent;
                    x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
                    y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
                }
            }

            VerticalLayout {
                alignment: center;
                spacing: 1px;
                horizontal-stretch: 1;

                Text {
                    text: root.working ? "Working…" : root.data.title;
                    color: Theme.text-primary;
                    font-size: Theme.fs-headline;
                    font-weight: Theme.fw-headline;
                    overflow: elide;
                }
                // Provenance sits under the title, always, in the dim colour metadata uses.
                // Not a chip and not a badge: it is not a thing to click, it is the answer to
                // "how do you know that".
                if root.data.source != "" : Text {
                    text: root.data.source;
                    color: Theme.text-dim;
                    font-size: Theme.fs-micro;
                    overflow: elide;
                }
            }
        }

        // ── The proposal ──
        if root.data.body != "" : Text {
            text: root.data.body;
            color: Theme.text-secondary;
            font-size: Theme.fs-caption;
            wrap: word-wrap;
        }

        // ── What pressing the button does ──
        //
        // Above the button, not beside it, and in the danger colour when it cannot be undone.
        // The person reads downward; the consequence should arrive before the control.
        if root.data.impact != "" : HorizontalLayout {
            spacing: Theme.sp-2;
            alignment: start;

            Icon {
                commands: root.data.destructive ? Icons.warning : Icons.arrow-right;
                size: 13px;
                tint: root.data.destructive ? Theme.color-danger : Theme.text-dim;
                y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
            }
            Text {
                text: root.data.impact;
                color: root.data.destructive ? Theme.color-danger : Theme.text-dim;
                font-size: Theme.fs-micro;
                vertical-alignment: center;
                overflow: elide;
            }
        }

        // ── The decision ──
        //
        // An empty impact means nothing changes: this is an answer to read, not a thing to
        // agree to, and it gets ONE button. Offering "Dismiss" and "Apply" for a summary that
        // applies nothing asks a question with no second answer — and Notes' own failure card
        // was doing exactly that, offering to apply an error message.
        HorizontalLayout {
            spacing: Theme.sp-2;
            alignment: end;

            if root.data.impact != "" : YButton {
                label: "Dismiss";
                size: 0;
                variant: 1;
                clicked => { root.dismiss(); }
            }
            if root.data.impact != "" : YButton {
                // The verb is the change. See the header.
                label: root.data.verb != "" ? root.data.verb : "Apply";
                size: 0;
                variant: root.data.destructive ? 3 : 0;
                // A change a mind proposes is a consent, like an approval: a click answers it,
                // Enter or Space on a focused button never does.
                pointer-only: true;
                disabled: root.working;
                loading: root.working;
                clicked => { root.apply(); }
            }
            if root.data.impact == "" : YButton {
                label: root.data.verb != "" ? root.data.verb : "Close";
                size: 0;
                variant: 1;
                clicked => { root.dismiss(); }
            }
        }
    }
}
